    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d390">

                <pb facs="0533" n="521" sameAs="frus:frus1946v08/pg521"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00119 PW/5–2246: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Political Adviser in Korea</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Langdon</hi>)<anchor n="44"
                        xml:id="d390-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi>
                        <lb/>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">us urgent</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1946-05-24T14:00:00-04:00">May 24, 1946—2 p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>77. For Ambassador Pauley. As you have seen from Department’s telegram to Moscow
                    repeated to Seoul as no. 76, May 22,<anchor n="45" xml:id="d390-f2"/> I have
                    again asked the Soviet Government to grant necessary authority for your Mission
                    to proceed to northern Korea. Since it appears that the size of your staff is
                    one of the principal obstacles in obtaining permission I hope you will make
                    every effort to reduce the number to a minimum adequate to your purposes.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Byrnes</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
